# Badger credentials. Copy to .env (gitignored) — never commit the filled copy.
# The runtime loads <agent-dir>/.env automatically, and it wins over
# ~/.gitagent/.env and over stale shell exports.
#
# Three variables are required. That is the whole list.

# --- model: Vertex AI on Application Default Credentials -------------------
# There is no model API key. Vertex authenticates through ADC, which means
# either `gcloud auth application-default login` on a laptop, or — in
# production — the service account attached to the VM, so nothing to deploy.
#
# All three of project, location, and working ADC are needed, or the run fails
# late and unclearly: gitagent has no pre-flight check for google-vertex.
GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=
GOOGLE_CLOUD_LOCATION=us-central1

# --- sources: Composio ------------------------------------------------------
# The only source credential. Badger authenticates to Composio; Composio holds
# the GitHub connection server-side, so no GitHub token is stored here.
# Get a key at https://app.composio.dev — free tier is 100k tool calls/month,
# hard-capped, no card.
#
# Connect GitHub once with:  npm run composio:connect
COMPOSIO_API_KEY=

# --- the demo gate (required before this is publicly reachable) -------------
# One shared passphrase, handed out alongside the URL. Not an account system:
# Badger has no per-user anything yet, so a real login would carry an identity
# the product cannot use. See the README on why this is a gate, not the product.
#
# With this UNSET the gate is off and the server refuses to bind to anything
# but 127.0.0.1 — fail safe rather than fail open. There is deliberately no
# default value.
BADGER_PASSPHRASE=

# Optional. Signs the session cookie. Set it so sessions survive a restart;
# leave it blank and a random key is generated at boot, which just means
# everyone signs in again after a deploy. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
BADGER_SESSION_SECRET=

# --- the agent's own repository (optional, and the learning loop needs it) --
# Where Badger's learned skills and self-written memory go, so that they
# outlive the container that produced them.
#
# Leave these unset and everything still works: the agent runs from the files
# in the image, learns during a session, and forgets at scale-to-zero. Set them
# and the agent runs from a clone of its own repo on a long-lived branch, and
# `skill_learner crystallize` becomes a commit a person can read and merge.
# See app/server/agent-repo.mjs and the README.
#
# This token is NOT a source credential and must never be one. It writes to
# Badger's own repository and nothing else — scope it to that single repo, with
# contents:write. Badger reads GitHub through Composio; see the block at the
# bottom on why GITHUB_TOKEN is still read by nothing.
# BADGER_AGENT_REPO_URL=https://github.com/owner/badger.git
# BADGER_AGENT_REPO_TOKEN=
#
# The one branch every run appends to. Never `main`: the agent commits to this
# and a human merges it, which is the review gate in OpenGAP's own
# human-in-the-loop pattern. Defaults to gitagent/learning.
# BADGER_LEARNING_BRANCH=gitagent/learning
#
# Where the clone lives. Defaults to <tmpdir>/badger-agent. On Cloud Run /tmp
# is a tmpfs and counts against the instance's memory, so the repo has to be
# small — Badger's is ~9MB of git objects.
# BADGER_AGENT_DIR=

# --- the database (optional; the product degrades without it) ---------------
# Postgres holds conversations, search history, the searchable copy of the
# corpus, and every scheduled run. It does NOT hold the agent: agent.yaml,
# SOUL.md, RULES.md, skills/ and memory/ stay as files in the git repo, which
# is the whole thesis of building on GAP.
#
# Leave it unset and everything still runs: history falls back to the browser
# and search falls back to the on-disk index, then to live federated queries.
# What you lose is anything that has to outlive the instance — including the
# record of what a schedule produced.
#
# Supabase: use the TRANSACTION POOLER string (port 6543), not the direct
# connection. The direct one is IPv6-only without the paid add-on and Cloud Run
# egresses over IPv4, so a direct URL works on a laptop and fails in production.
# DATABASE_URL=

# --- scheduling -------------------------------------------------------------
# The credential for POST /api/schedules/tick, which is the one route outside
# the passphrase gate — Cloud Scheduler cannot hold a session cookie — and the
# one that spends the answer budget. Sent as an x-badger-tick header and
# compared constant-time.
#
# UNSET the endpoint refuses everything: it fails closed, because the
# alternative is an open endpoint that costs money. Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
# BADGER_TICK_SECRET=

# Development only. Runs an in-process timer that checks every minute, because
# there is no Cloud Scheduler on a laptop — without it a local schedule saves,
# shows its next run, and never fires, which looks exactly like a bug.
#
# NEVER set this in production. There it would be a second trigger beside Cloud
# Scheduler, and two things deciding whether a schedule is due is the shape
# that ends in double runs.
# BADGER_LOCAL_TICK=1

# --- optional ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Which repository Badger is scoped to. Defaults to alanmathews9/arkind-internal.
# Which repository GitHub search reads. REQUIRED for the GitHub source —
# there is no default, because a repo name only means something to the key
# whose connection can read it. Gmail and Drive need no equivalent.
BADGER_GITHUB_REPO=owner/name
#
# End-user id for the Composio session. One user for now; in a multi-user
# product this is the logged-in user's id. Note the known limit: it is read by
# tool subprocesses from the environment, so it races under concurrent users.
# A label inside the Composio workspace your key opens; connections made by
# `npm run connect` attach to it. Any name works ("default" when unset).
# BADGER_USER_ID=default
#
# Port for `npm run serve`. Defaults to 4000 — 3333 is gitagent's own UI.
# BADGER_PORT=4000
#
# Spend and abuse limits. Answers cost real Vertex credits and Composio quota,
# so they are capped per IP (15/hour), globally per day, and by concurrency.
# How often the running server refreshes the local search index with
# changes from the sources (incremental, ~10-20 API calls a tick). Hours;
# 0 disables. A missing index still builds itself at boot either way.
# BADGER_INDEX_REFRESH_HOURS=2

# BADGER_DAILY_ASK_LIMIT=250
# BADGER_MAX_CONCURRENT_ASKS=3
#
# Set to 0 ONLY for local http development — it drops Secure from the session
# cookie. Never set this on a deployed instance.
# BADGER_SECURE_COOKIE=1

# ============================================================================
# NOT required. Listed so nobody re-adds them.
#
#   GITHUB_TOKEN            Nothing reads it, and it is kept that way on
#                           purpose. Composio holds the GitHub credential for
#                           the SOURCES; the MCP server that needed a token is
#                           gone, and agent.yaml has no mcp_servers block.
#                           The runtime would fall back to this name for repo
#                           mode (dist/sdk.js:101) and app/server/agent-repo.mjs
#                           deliberately does not, so that a write token for the
#                           agent's own repo can never be mistaken for — or
#                           quietly reused as — a token that reaches a source.
#   GEMINI_API_KEY          AI Studio free tier caps at 5 requests/minute.
#   GROQ_API_KEY            Groq free tier caps at 8k tokens/minute, below what
#                           one turn needs. Both replaced by Vertex on ADC.
#   GOOGLE_OAUTH_CREDENTIALS  Gmail and Drive are connected, but through
#                           Composio like GitHub — it holds those credentials
#                           server-side, so no Google OAuth secret lives here.
#   GITAGENT_USERNAME       For gitagent's own bundled web UI, which Badger
#   GITAGENT_PASSWORD       does not use — its composer is broken and it
#                           switches the repo onto a chat/<timestamp> branch.
# ============================================================================
